EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DAWN #6B#

A grey jenny stands loaded with two leather bags; the silver is sewn into them. Mist in the valley. Millstones in the house.

FLASH. A purse tipped over an open palm; a single coin falls out.

Miriam presses a round of bread into Asher's hands.

MIRIAM
Eat it slowly. There is no oven on that road.

Eliab lays his hands on his son's head. Asher's eyes close.

ELIAB
The LORD bless you, and keep you.

ELIAB
The LORD make his face to shine upon you, and be gracious to you.

ELIAB
The LORD lift up his countenance upon you, and give you peace.

ELIAB
Be strong and of good courage. The LORD your God is with you wherever you go.

ELIAB
The lamp is lit at every dark.

Asher opens his eyes.

ASHER
Save your oil.

Eliab's face goes still. His hand grips the gatepost.

Asher takes the halter and goes out of the gate. The dog starts after him.

ASHER
Stay. Keep the flock. You always did.

The dog stays, and sits at Eliab's feet.

EXT. THE ROAD - THE BEND - MORNING

Amos catches up with Asher at the bend, his own cloak over his arm.

AMOS
You will be cold in the hills beyond the Jordan.

He holds it out. Asher takes it.

AMOS
Father would want you warm.

A short, rough embrace. Amos holds him stiffly. Over his brother's shoulder he sees the sling wound round Asher's wrist.

AMOS
I plaited that for you when you could barely lift a stone.

ASHER
(trying for a joke)
Keep my ewe fat. I will want her for my wedding.

It dies. Amos says nothing.

Asher goes on down the road with the jenny. Amos stands at the bend and watches until his brother is a speck, and then nothing.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DUSK

Eliab strikes a light with his flint and lights the lamp in the niche, alone. The dog lies at his feet.

He puts his hand flat on the stone beside the flame, and weeps without a sound.

Then he wipes his face with his sleeve and turns to the yard.

ELIAB
Shallum! The oxen have not been watered.

